Chapter: Codex Types

 Type representations used by the type checker and later stages.

Section: Primitive Types
  CodexType =
   | IntegerTy (Integer) (Integer) (OverflowMode)
   | RealTy (RealWidth) (RealMode)
   | TextTy
   | BooleanTy
   | CharTy
   | VoidTy
   | NothingTy
   | ErrorTy
   | FunTy (CodexType) (EffectRow) (CodexType)
   | ListTy (CodexType)
   | LinkedListTy (CodexType)
   | TypeVar (Integer between 0 and 4294967295)
   | ForAllTy (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) (CodexType)
   | SumTy (Name) (List CodexType) (List SumCtor)
   | RecordTy (Name) (List CodexType) (List RecordField)
   | ConstructedTy (Name) (List CodexType)
   | EffectfulTy (List Name) (List Text) (CodexType)
   | ProofTy
   | PropEqTy (CodexType) (CodexType)
   | UnitTy (Name) (CodexType)
   | VectorTy (Integer) (CodexType)
   | VectorMaskTy (Integer)
   | TypeCon (Name)
   | TypeApply (CodexType) (CodexType)
   | ForAllEff (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) (CodexType)
   | LinearTy (CodexType)

  SumCtor = record {
   name : Name,
   fields : List CodexType,
   return-type : List CodexType
  }

  RecordField = record {
   name : Name,
   type-val : CodexType
  }

Section: Effect Rows

 The effect row on an arrow. A row is a set of effect
 labels plus an optional row variable. tail-name is the source-level
 lowercase identifier, "" when the row was not written with a tail.
 tail-id is the numeric row-variable id: -1 means no id (a closed
 row, or a source tail not yet parameterized); ids at 0 and above are
 assigned at signature parameterization from the unifier's shared
 32-bit counter, bound under a ForAllEff binder, freshened at
 instantiation, and solved against the unifier's row-substitution
 table. Rows are deliberately not CodexTypes: a type position can
 never resolve to a row, so a kind confusion is unrepresentable.
 Labels are kept name-then-scope sorted and duplicate-free by
 construction; rows are idempotent sets, never multisets. Through
 stage 2 rows remain inert -- concrete effects still ride in
 EffectfulTy until stage 3 migrates them onto the arrow and enforces
 the row at declared boundaries. All pure arrows share the single
 interned empty-row; do not allocate a fresh empty row per arrow.

  EffectLabel = record {
   name : Name,
   scope : Text
  }

  EffectRow = record {
   labels : List EffectLabel,
   tail-name : Text,
   tail-id : Integer between -1 and 4294967295
  }

  empty-row : EffectRow = EffectRow { labels = [], tail-name = "", tail-id = __narrow (-1) }

  tail-only-row : Text -> EffectRow
  tail-only-row (name) = EffectRow { labels = [], tail-name = name, tail-id = __narrow (-1) }

  row-var : Integer -> EffectRow
  row-var (id) = EffectRow { labels = [], tail-name = "", tail-id = __narrow id }

  concrete-row : Text -> EffectRow
  concrete-row (name) = EffectRow { labels = [EffectLabel { name = Name { value = name }, scope = "" }], tail-name = "", tail-id = __narrow (-1) }

  row-with-tail-id : EffectRow, Integer -> EffectRow
  row-with-tail-id (row) (id) = EffectRow { labels = row.labels, tail-name = row.tail-name, tail-id = __narrow id }

  row-is-empty : EffectRow -> Boolean
  row-is-empty (row) = list-length (row.labels) == 0 & text-length (row.tail-name) == 0 & row.tail-id < 0

  row-is-open : EffectRow -> Boolean
  row-is-open (row) = row.tail-id >= 0

Section: Overflow Modes

 The behavior of a narrow-store at a bounded-Integer field when the
 incoming value falls outside the field's declared range. Set on a
 type at declaration time; consulted by codegen at every
 narrow-store site for that type. Mode is irrelevant for full-width
 (`i64-min..i64-max`) integers -- narrow-store does not fire.

  OverflowMode =
   | OvError
   | OvWrapping
   | OvClamping

Section: Real Width And Mode

 Precision and safety are INDEPENDENT choices and the type used to carry
 exactly one of them, so of the six combinations only four could be
 spelled and both missing ones were f32. They are two fields here for
 that reason rather than for symmetry with IntegerTy.

 RmDefault is IEEE 754: an overflow yields an infinity and a caller who
 wants to know must ask. RmTrapping halts instead. RmSaturating clamps
 to the largest finite value of the width, so the answer stays a number
 the next operation can use.

  RealWidth =
   | RwF64
   | RwF32

  RealMode =
   | RmDefault
   | RmTrapping
   | RmSaturating

 The spellings that existed before the collapse, named once so the
 hundred-odd mentions across the compiler do not each spell out a pair.
 `int-ty-default` is the same idea for integers and the precedent.

  real-f64 : CodexType = RealTy RwF64 RmDefault
  real-f32 : CodexType = RealTy RwF32 RmDefault
  real-f64-trapping : CodexType = RealTy RwF64 RmTrapping
  real-f64-saturating : CodexType = RealTy RwF64 RmSaturating
  real-f32-trapping : CodexType = RealTy RwF32 RmTrapping
  real-f32-saturating : CodexType = RealTy RwF32 RmSaturating

Section: Integer Bounds
  i64-max : Integer = 9223372036854775807

  i64-min : Integer = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

  int-ty-default : CodexType = IntegerTy i64-min i64-max OvError

Section: Unit Stripping

 A `unit` type is a wrapper codegen erases, so anything dispatching on what
 a value IS at runtime has to look through it. This is that one look, and it
 lives here rather than in a back end because it is a question about the
 type, not about a machine.

 It recurses, because nothing prevents a unit wrapping a unit.

 There were four hand-maintained answers to this question and they did not
 agree. `emit-ord-comparison` stripped recursively; `is-fusable-int-comparison`
 and `cmp-answers-operand` stripped one level; `lower-bin-op` stripped nothing
 at all, so every arithmetic operator on a `unit Real` fell past the floating
 arms to the integer one and added two IEEE-754 bit patterns. That is wrong on
 every input rather than on a quarter of them, and it emitted no diagnostic
 because `CDX9010` guards the comparison emitter and arithmetic does not go
 through it.

 The lesson is the one this tree keeps relearning: a predicate copied is a
 predicate that will drift. Derive it once and let every caller read it.

  strip-unit-ty : CodexType -> CodexType
  strip-unit-ty (ty) =
   when ty
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> strip-unit-ty inner
    is otherwise -> ty

Section: Real Mode Classification

  is-real-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  is-real-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  is-real-f32 : CodexType -> Boolean
  is-real-f32 (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> is-width-f32 w
    is otherwise -> False

  is-width-f32 : RealWidth -> Boolean
  is-width-f32 (w) =
   when w
    is RwF32 -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  real-mode-of : CodexType -> RealMode
  real-mode-of (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> m
    is otherwise -> RmDefault

  real-width-of : CodexType -> RealWidth
  real-width-of (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> w
    is otherwise -> RwF64

 A variant carries no structural equality, so the two fields need their
 own comparisons before a unifier can ask whether two Real types agree.

  real-width-eq : RealWidth, RealWidth -> Boolean
  real-width-eq (a) (b) = is-width-f32 a == is-width-f32 b

  real-mode-eq : RealMode, RealMode -> Boolean
  real-mode-eq (a) (b) = real-mode-rank a == real-mode-rank b

  real-mode-rank : RealMode -> Integer
  real-mode-rank (m) =
   when m
    is RmDefault -> 0
    is RmTrapping -> 1
    is RmSaturating -> 2

  real-mode-is-trapping : RealMode -> Boolean
  real-mode-is-trapping (m) =
   when m
    is RmTrapping -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  real-mode-is-saturating : RealMode -> Boolean
  real-mode-is-saturating (m) =
   when m
    is RmSaturating -> True
    is otherwise -> False

Section: Pointer Classification

 Whether a runtime value of this type is a heap pointer (True) or an
 inline scalar (False). The foundation for precise pointer-maps: the
 escape check, dead-deck reclamation, and a copying compactor all need
 to know which record/ctor slots hold pointers vs raw values.

 Scalars are stored inline: Integer (1/2/4/8 bytes by bounds), Boolean
 (1), Char and Number (8, but not pointers). Everything heap-allocated
 is an 8-byte pointer. Nullary ctors are heap-boxed too (emit-nullary-ctor
 allocates 8 bytes and stores the tag), so ALL SumTy values are pointers.
 EffectfulTy, ForAllTy, ForAllEff, and LinearTy classify by their
 underlying value type. LinearTy is the stage-1 ownership marker:
 it survives resolution so later stages
 can read declared linearity, but it is representation-transparent
 everywhere -- width, layout, unification, and the IR wire all see
 the inner type.

 TypeVar is the one undecidable case: a polymorphic field's runtime
 representation depends on its instantiation. Classified conservatively
 as a pointer here; a precise map for a generic record requires a
 per-instantiation (monomorphized) map, which is future work.

  is-pointer-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  is-pointer-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) | RealTy (rw) (rm) | BooleanTy | CharTy | VoidTy | NothingTy | ErrorTy | ProofTy | PropEqTy (a) (b) -> False
    is TextTy | ListTy (e) | LinkedListTy (e) | RecordTy (n) (a) (f) | SumTy (n) (a) (c) | ConstructedTy (n) (a) | FunTy (p) (row) (r) | VectorTy (vn) (vt) | VectorMaskTy (mn) -> True
    is EffectfulTy (e) (s) (r) -> is-pointer-type r
    is ForAllTy (i) (b) -> is-pointer-type b
    is ForAllEff (i) (b) -> is-pointer-type b
    is TypeVar (i) -> True
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> is-pointer-type inner
    is TypeCon (n) -> False
    is TypeApply (f) (a) -> True
    is LinearTy (inner) -> is-pointer-type inner

